  "title": "CHAPTER 2: GEOGRAPHY OF PUNJAB",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "apei287",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.1 Location and Extent"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Punjab is located in northwestern India, bordered by Jammu & Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Haryana, Rajasthan, and Pakistan.",
        "The physical landscape comprises the Shivalik Hills, the Piedmont Plains (Kandi tract), and the vast Alluvial Plains.",
        "The principal rivers flowing through Indian Punjab are the Sutlej, Beas, and Ravi.",
        "Inter-river land tracts called Doabs form the agricultural backbone of the state, notably Bist Doab and Bari Doab.",
        "Irrigation is primarily served by an extensive network of canals, including the Sirhind Canal and Upper Bari Doab Canal, alongside tube wells."
      ],
      "id": "d84mlk9"
    },
